  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nThe document named-property algorithms include `<object>` and `<embed>` elements only when they are [exposed](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#exposed). For `<object>`, exposedness depends on fallback-content state, nested `<object>`/`<embed>` descendants, and recursively on object ancestors.\n\nNo browser implements this consistently:\n\n- Gecko ignores exposedness for both named-property lookup and supported property names.\n- Blink and WebKit ignore fallback state and use only the nesting-based approximation for lookup.\n- Blink and WebKit nevertheless include unexposed objects/embeds in supported property names, so `Object.getOwnPropertyNames(document)` can include a name for which lookup produces no value.\n\nConsequently, the valid nested-`<object>` assertions in [the WPT](https://wpt.fyi/results/html/dom/documents/dom-tree-accessors/nameditem-names.html) fail in all browsers.\n\nI suggest we remove exposedness filtering from document named properties and follow Gecko’s simpler behavior. Blink and WebKit's behavior makes no sense as-is, and the complexity of choosing a corrected version of their behavior doesn't seem worth it for these legacy features.",
